WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.290
Implementing the Recommendations of the Francis
Report and the Winterbourne View Report

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:08.620
Bill McCarthy and Jane Cummings
National Director of Policy, Chief Nursing

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.509
Officer
Introductory remarks

00:00:10.509 --> 00:00:14.290
Commonality
Bill McCarthy: Thank you, Chairman.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:21.640
Yes, Jane and I share the executive lead on
this, but I will introduce and hand across

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:22.640
to Jane.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:30.600
None of us need reminding that both the Francis
Report into Mid Staffordshire and the Winterbourne

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:43.260
View Report highlighted some appalling failures
in care and I think marked a really important

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:49.050
stage in us rethinking the way we relate to
citizens, to people and to patients in the

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:53.769
delivery and the shaping of care and services.

00:00:53.769 --> 00:01:01.640
We have deliberately brought together our
reporting on Francis and Winterbourne View;

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:09.850
the reason for that is that there is a risk
that the Francis Report and the publicity

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:15.610
around that crowds out some of the really
important lessons from Winterbourne View,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.610
as well.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.610
We believe there is an awful lot of commonality
across those two.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:29.310
In the way we are looking to change services
and improve care, we want to keep the lessons

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.110
from both very close together.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:37.960
Wider context
I think the primary lesson is about the culture

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:45.659
change that is required in services, better
to provide high quality care for all, now

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:47.620
and for future generations.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:55.780
Indeed, in the way in which our Board has
discussed this before and continues to talk

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:02.060
about these, we have tried to draw these lessons
into the whole of our business plan: not so

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:07.850
that we have a discreet set of actions in
one area, but we have tried to use it to inform

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.130
the whole way we work.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:19.390
Everything Tim was talking about on openness,
engagement and involvement of patients is

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:25.270
central to the sort of culture change which
we think is fundamental to really hearing

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.560
and responding to the lessons of these reports.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.250
Actions
We have also committed to undertake a set

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:39.920
of specific actions and we promised previously
that we would report on those very openly

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.480
and transparently, both to the Board and to
the public in general and that is what the

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.450
annexes in this report do.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:54.470
They set out that we are making progress across
the breadth of our commitments.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:03:01.290
I think I would highlight that, particularly
around a number of the Winterbourne View commitments,

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:07.980
we have developed an exceptionally strong
working relationship with local governments.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:16.200
Indeed, many of these we are jointly implementing
and I should mention Chris Bull, who is an

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.290
ex local authority chief executive who is
now working across local government and ourselves

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.730
to give some real leadership to that.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:30.250
I should also mention the political leadership
of Norman Lamb, who has been and continues

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:36.760
to be a strong advocate for the lessons of
Winterbourne View and that particular group

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.570
of people who are disadvantaged and can be
left out if they do not have someone very

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.940
publically voicing their interests.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:48.230
We have worked very closely with the Minister
in this work.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:53.580
Conclusions
The message, I think, is a reassuring one

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:59.900
for the Board, in that we are making progress
across all of our commitments; but I would

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:06.680
not want to underestimate how much there is
still to do.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:13.410
We can undertake a set of actions, but to
change the NHS will take longer.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:19.310
We are, however, absolutely determined and
we are keeping to all of our commitments.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:25.620
Jane may want to pick up more of the specifics
or give a particular angle from Compassion

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.000
in Practice, which is a key part of this.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.920
Fundamental change
Jane Cummings: I think Bill said a significant

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.060
amount of what we needed to cover in this
report.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:38.180
I think, from my point of view, that there
are a couple of things which I would want

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.180
to reiterate.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.590
One is that this is absolutely about a fundamental
change in the way we approach care: this is

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.720
about the culture of the NHS more generally.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.830
It reinforces the fact that this is about
what we are, this is about what we do: this

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:57.080
is about our value basis, this is about how
we change the way in which we both provide

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.310
care and really concentrate.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:03.930
We actually live the values.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:07.070
We talk about them; it is not just about talk,
it is about what happens at the front line.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:11.840
It is about what happens in the way we both
commission and support people to do the right

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:13.870
thing for our patients.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.370
Compassion in Practice
Compassion in Practice, which we will come

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:22.280
back to at a future Board meeting, has really
been set up to transform the way nurses, midwives

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:27.760
and care staff do what they need to do; but
it is also about the values of the six Cs

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.630
that we have talked about in the Board meeting
before, which are being taken up by all staff,

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:35.190
in all settings, across the NHS.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:40.080
That has been really positive for me as I
have gone round the country and looked at

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.080
how people are doing things.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.610
Winterbourne View
In terms of this particular report, I just

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:48.560
wanted to highlight the additional work we
are doing around Winterbourne View.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:55.190
We have gone a step further and are now looking
in quite a lot of detail at the quality of

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.240
some of the work which is being done.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:02.440
It is not just about ticking the box and saying,
'Yes, we have one it; we have met those recommendations.'

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:09.590
We have gone back in with an expert in learning
disability care, working alongside our stakeholders

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:14.380
to assess: has the work that has been done
been of good enough quality; can we do a deep

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:20.950
dive into some of the detailed work; there
any variation in the way in which this is

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:26.000
being done across the country; can we improve
that; can we make sure that we have made it

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.770
the best it possibly can be and looked at
individual needs for patients?

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:33.500
Detailed response
I wanted to flag that but also to highlight

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:41.820
that our detailed response to the 290 recommendations
that were in Francis will be done in collaboration

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:49.370
with our key partners and we will also look
at the Berwick Review, the Cavendish review,

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:54.190
the work that Tricia Hart and Ann Clwyd have
done on complaints, when that is published

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:59.280
and Reducing the Burden of Bureaucracy, which
is also due to be published soon.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.920
We will look at all of those together in terms
of our overall response.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:07.460
I think, however, that Bill has said a significant
amount of what we wanted to cover and I am

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:12.880
very happy that between us we can answer any
questions that other Board Members may have.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.810
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: What is common between those

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:23.690
two appalling instances and perhaps more accentuated
in Winterbourne View was the sheer vulnerability

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:25.190
of the patients.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:29.950
There was no one there to support them against
that very cruel treatment.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:34.810
All the things which we have discussed this
morning are instances where patients have

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:43.510
a positive feedback, through all the initiatives
that Tim has outlined; where patients are

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:49.360
vulnerable and not able to input feedback
back into the system, how can we be absolutely

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.330
sure that they are getting the highest quality
care?

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:57.169
Jane Cummings: There are a variety of ways
of doing that.

00:07:57.169 --> 00:08:03.270
If you look at the work we have done on Compassion
in Practice, for example, we have six different

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:07.561
areas of action and I would highlight a couple
of them.

00:08:07.561 --> 00:08:11.570
One is around the patient experience and we
have heard a lot about FFT.

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:15.850
One of the pieces of work we are doing at
the moment with the patient experience domain

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:21.660
is how we can ensure that those most vulnerable
people, who may find it difficult to answer

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:25.430
the FFT type question, can have their voices
heard.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:30.699
Neil Churchill, who is a Director of Patient
Experience who leads work on improving patient

00:08:30.699 --> 00:08:37.789
experience, is spending time currently looking
at how we can truly access the real feedback

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:41.380
that the most vulnerable give; those with
dementia, those with a learning disability

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:45.720
and those children and young people who find
it more difficult to answer the FFT.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.190
We are looking at that.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:52.140
The second area that I wanted to highlight
is the action that looks at measuring impact

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.320
of care.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.339
How do we measure the things that really matter?

00:08:55.339 --> 00:09:02.040
How do we demonstrate how we are making improvements
in terms of true measures of quality that

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:07.360
may include for our patients things like falls,
pressure ulcers and healthcare associated

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:08.360
infections?

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:11.190
We are looking at those sorts of things together.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:15.511
The next area is: how do we build and strengthen
the leadership of our staff, so that they

00:09:15.511 --> 00:09:21.170
are in a position where they can lead those
services, they can feed back and they have

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:28.700
the time and the scope to be able really to
spend concentrated effort with patients and

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:31.380
understand what is happening on a day by day
basis?

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.040
The other one I wanted really to highlight
is our staff experience.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:41.860
If you listen to staff and you listen to patients,
we understand so much better what is going

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.060
on.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:49.170
Asking staff whether the area they work in
is one in which they would want their loved

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:54.700
ones to be cared for, whether they feel that
they are giving good quality care and are

00:09:54.700 --> 00:10:00.350
able to give the quality of care which is
deserved or needed by patients and lastly,

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:07.300
whether they would recommend that place as
a place to work, speaks volumes about care.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.029
It is not one simple issue.

00:10:10.029 --> 00:10:14.740
We need to build those things together in
order really to tell the story.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:18.600
We need to hold the mirror up and say, 'Is
this care good?

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.950
Do our patients say it is good?

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:22.700
What do our patients say we need to do?'

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:28.820
We need to do that at a level that is detailed
enough that in individual wards and in some

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:33.300
cases, individual shift by shift -- whether
that be in hospital or out in the community

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:34.810
-- is absolutely key.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:37.920
There is a lot of work we are doing to try
to pull that together.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.550
Certainly, Compassion in Practice is driving
a lot of that, but we need to think about

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:47.210
and we are thinking about the fact that this
is not just about nursing, midwifery and care

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:51.690
staff, this is about the whole team: this
is about doctors, it is about AHPs, it is

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:55.839
about the reception staff in GP surgeries.

00:10:55.839 --> 00:11:01.029
It is about looking at everybody in terms
of how we ensure that that care is the best

00:11:01.029 --> 00:11:02.529
it possibly can be.

00:11:02.529 --> 00:11:04.029
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:11:04.029 --> 00:11:09.610
These were not occasional acts; these were
instances of institutionalised cruelty, which

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:13.750
we would never have believed could have occurred
in the NHS.

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:15.920
We cannot be complacent ever again about that.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:23.440
It is just so essential to ensure that there
is no repetition of what we saw in those two

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:24.440
instances.

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:29.210
I am reassured by this but we, as a Board,
will seek constant reassurance that nothing

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:33.700
escapes our overview of what needs to occur.

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:36.050
I have Bill and Victor to come in.

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.050
Please.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:38.050
Bill McCarthy: Thank you.

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:40.810
I have to say, in response to your question,
Malcolm, I think the greatest risk which has

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:51.880
been there over the years is this, 'Out of
sight, out of mind' mind set for some of the

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:54.270
most vulnerable people.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:12:03.510
We are talking about around 1,300 people who
have been in in patient beds for learning

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:04.510
disability or autism.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:15.410
That is not a huge number when divided across
the 200 plus clinical commissioning groups

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:23.550
and what we are trying to do through this
programme is not have them, if you like, on

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:32.890
the end of an annual commissioning decision,
but to be in a continuing relationship with

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:43.930
them, their families and their carers of support
and oversight of the conditions; and continuing

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:49.800
to make sure that they are getting the right
care, which best suits them, in the right

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:50.800
environment.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:58.800
The enemy here is, 'Out of sight, out of mind',
so the visibility of knowing who those people

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:05.180
are all the time, understanding their situations
and working with their families and themselves

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:09.360
to describe what will best suit them is absolutely
the key.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:13.890
Ongoing relationships are part of what we
need to do for those most vulnerable people.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.910
Victor Adebowale: I think there are two things.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:22.120
The first is that while I welcome everything
that has been said to date, I think Malcolm's

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:25.770
point goes to the heart of the issue.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:31.920
Communicating with a number of people with
learning disabilities and dementia and other

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:38.540
things is about our understanding of the nature
of their communication difficulties.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:45.950
If you look at these cases of continuous abuse,
they fed on the fact that these individuals

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:52.260
could not communicate and that there was no
means by which the system could communicate

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:53.260
with them.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:54.260
There are two things.

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:59.050
One is: I am interested in what work we are
doing with organisations outside of the NHS

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:00.050
family.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:04.300
I am aware that in the not for profit world
there is a collection of organisations that

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:10.550
are focused on Winterbourne View and the response
and how that changes culture and communication

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:12.130
in a systematic way.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:13.709
I think we should understand that.

00:14:13.709 --> 00:14:18.340
I am not expecting that we should all have
a year's training in Makaton, but we should

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:22.491
understand how we are communicating with those
people as much as we understand how we are

00:14:22.491 --> 00:14:25.630
communicating with people who do not have
learning disabilities or who speak English,

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:29.839
or who do not speak English, for that matter.

00:14:29.839 --> 00:14:35.620
As such, I think we should understand a bit
more about what the basic methodology of communication

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:40.310
that we are using is; and secondly, how we
are working across the piece with organisations

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:46.840
that are outside our immediate purview, but
with whom these people have day to day contact

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:51.190
-- with organisations in the not for profit
sector, organisations in the private sector,

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:57.640
etc. -- and whether we can build a much more
joined up consensus as to what should be happening,

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:01.920
how we know it is happening and how these
people can be given a voice in deciding what

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:02.920
should happen.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:11.310
Jane Cummings: Tim might want to talk a bit
more about the work his team are doing around

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:13.290
patient and public engagement.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:19.750
I think you make a really good point, Victor
and I think we need to be upfront about the

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:22.290
fact that we still need to do more.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:29.520
Certainly, in my team we have just appointed
the Head of Mental Health and Learning Disability;

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:33.890
it took us some time to get the right person
appointed to that.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:39.829
Neil Churchill has also just, in the last
couple of weeks, completed his team; and within

00:15:39.829 --> 00:15:44.709
that, he also has somebody with a specific
responsibility around mental health and learning

00:15:44.709 --> 00:15:45.709
disability.

00:15:45.709 --> 00:15:50.360
I think what is very clear from the work which
Neil and others have done is that he has spent

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:55.350
a considerable amount of time engaging with
other sectors -- the third sector, the charitable

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:59.420
sector, the voluntary sector -- and I think
we will start to see the benefits of that

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:01.829
work over the next few months.

00:16:01.829 --> 00:16:05.360
We have, however, got further to go, there
is absolutely no doubt about that; and we

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:11.112
need to ensure that we concentrate on being
able to communicate best with those who are

00:16:11.112 --> 00:16:14.880
most vulnerable and with those who cannot
communicate in the way in which we would otherwise

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:15.880
do.

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:16.880
In the part, I have learned Makaton.

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:19.600
Whether I could still do it, I am not sure.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:23.350
I do think, though, that we need to think
differently and I do think we need to come

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:27.720
back to this and be able to assure you in
more detail in the future.

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:32.170
I know, however, that Tim's team also have
a significant amount of work around engaging,

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:34.190
so he might want to add to that.

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:35.820
Tim Kelsey: Yes, very quickly.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:39.610
I mentioned the Citizens' Assembly and of
course, Victor, you are leading on that.

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:43.870
I think that will have a very important role
to play in bringing people together.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:52.340
You are so right to say that the NHS has underexploited
the incredible value other partners in the

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:56.990
third sector and frankly, also private sector
partners, in delivering innovative new ways

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:04.860
of having better conversations with people;
but also to empower them to be able to speak

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.120
more clearly to us, as well, in a sense.

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:09.009
There is that.

00:17:09.009 --> 00:17:13.360
We are publishing -- as we are statutorily
required to do -- guidance to the commissioning

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:19.139
system as a whole, of course including us,
on how best to improve the proper degree of

00:17:19.139 --> 00:17:24.569
participation that they are promoting in development
of their commissioning plan.

00:17:24.569 --> 00:17:29.080
In there, there are a whole variety of different
standards -- I will not go through them all

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:34.779
now -- we are setting for how we believe people
will have to work much harder on collaborative

00:17:34.779 --> 00:17:39.570
engagement with organisations in other sectors
altogether.

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:40.890
I think there is a lot of work going on.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:45.049
I would be very happy to bring back to the
Board more details on that at some point soon,

00:17:45.049 --> 00:17:51.210
but there is a lot going on.

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:55.440
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:56.869
I think the Board will want to keep that under
continuous review.

00:17:56.869 --> 00:17:57.869
Ciarán?

00:17:57.869 --> 00:17:58.869
Ciarán Devane: I think I can see the work
we are doing to the system now and transparency

00:17:58.869 --> 00:17:59.879
and so on and I think I can see the work we
are doing through professions; but we keep

00:17:59.879 --> 00:18:01.840
talking about having three layers of defence.

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.320
There are boards of Trusts.

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:10.539
What are we doing to encourage CCGs to bounce
into their local provider and say, 'Are you,

00:18:10.539 --> 00:18:14.499
as the governance of this organisation...?';
what are we doing about talking to the TDA

00:18:14.499 --> 00:18:17.899
and what are we doing about talking to Monitor
to tell those bodies we have an expectation

00:18:17.899 --> 00:18:20.169
that they are part of this, as well?

00:18:20.169 --> 00:18:21.440
That middle layer I am not hearing.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:23.020
Tim Kelsey: That is what the guidance is.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:28.450
The guidance, specifically, is describing
the standard, which we are legally required

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:34.950
to set and then, through assurance, to make
sure is delivered, for CCGs and ourselves

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:38.700
as direct commissioners in relation to participation
and engagement.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:44.269
It is specifically saying to boards of CCGs,
to area teams and to ourselves what standards

00:18:44.269 --> 00:18:47.690
we think need to be achieved in relation to
this.

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:53.270
We are about to publish that and that will,
hopefully, at least provide a kind of baseline

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:58.519
against which people can judge how to do better
on collaborative engagement across the piece,

00:18:58.519 --> 00:19:01.071
not just in relation to this.

00:19:01.071 --> 00:19:06.059
That covers all sorts of things like partnership
working, as we as raising new standards around

00:19:06.059 --> 00:19:08.220
personal care planning, which I think will
be another route to it.

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:12.350
Ciarán Devane: I am sure it will be great
guidance, but how do we know that the Board

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:19.999
of a particular Trust in any town has actually
read it and is actually making sure that they

00:19:19.999 --> 00:19:20.999
are worrying about this.

00:19:20.999 --> 00:19:23.419
Tim Kelsey: If they are a CCG, we go in and
assure them on the basis of it.

00:19:23.419 --> 00:19:26.840
That will be published and we have a duty
to assure, if they are a CCG.

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:28.950
Now, if they are a provider Trust, I agree
that is an issue.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:34.030
Although we can tell a Hospital Trust what
we think is good practice, I do not have a

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:36.730
mandate to enforce it, other than through
commissioning.

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:41.259
Personally, however, I believe that this is
about commissioning and I think it will be

00:19:41.259 --> 00:19:45.019
really important for us and for CCGs, when
they approach their commissioning strategies,

00:19:45.019 --> 00:19:51.269
to recognise that outcomes also include the
degree to which people are actively engaging

00:19:51.269 --> 00:19:53.340
and actively encouraging participation.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:55.070
That is our principal lever.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:20:01.190
Jane Cummings: Ciarán has made a really good
point.

00:20:01.190 --> 00:20:04.570
Very briefly, there are two things that I
can do.

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:09.169
One is that as Chief Nursing Officer for England,
I have a professional leadership responsibility

00:20:09.169 --> 00:20:15.179
and role with all nurses and midwives, including
those who work in providers, so I do get invited

00:20:15.179 --> 00:20:19.749
in and I do visit and spend time; and we have,
through the Six Cs live website, the ability

00:20:19.749 --> 00:20:26.169
to look at that best practice, as well as
supporting CCGs to be able to do that through

00:20:26.169 --> 00:20:27.169
their commissioning role.

00:20:27.169 --> 00:20:31.739
The other thing I do is chair a federation
of nurse leaders.

00:20:31.739 --> 00:20:38.429
I chair a group which includes the nurse leaders
in Health Education England, Public Health

00:20:38.429 --> 00:20:43.950
England, the Department of Health and NTDA,
as well as through NHS England's route.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:51.669
Through that, we have the ability to work
with and alongside those professional leads

00:20:51.669 --> 00:20:55.710
in places like CQC, Monitor and all of the
other arms length bodies.

00:20:55.710 --> 00:21:00.129
That, we have found, has been a really powerful
way not only of talking to each other but

00:21:00.129 --> 00:21:04.229
sharing practice and understanding what some
of those regulators are also doing with their

00:21:04.229 --> 00:21:06.970
organisations, many of which are providers.

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:08.929
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:21:08.929 --> 00:21:13.869
Francis was a wake up call to the system and
I think we want to make sure the system does

00:21:13.869 --> 00:21:18.132
not go back to sleep, so we will continue
to monitor this very closely in the Board.

00:21:18.132 --> 00:21:19.132
May we now move on, please, to the next item,
which is the relentless advance of winter

00:21:19.132 --> 00:21:20.132
and invite Barbara Hakin to speak to the paper.

00:21:20.132 --> 00:21:20.133
Barbara?

